thank you for being there like a mom  and supporting me through some of the  toughest times you will be missed  this surprise party in 2019 was the last  birthday  jonathan farrington spent with his  beloved mother dawn  before she embarked on a new life  my mom was to put us simply my best  friend  dawn lived apart from her husband frank  galenchen for a decade  while she worked at a long-term care  home in oshawa ontario  he built their retirement home in the  beautiful seaside village of  port-au-prince nova scotia  when she retired they were finally  together living their dream  when she got down there you know as hard  as it was for all of us  it was the best moment of her life i'm  sure  but that moment didn't last less than a  year later  tragedy struck good evening thank you  for joining us on what has been an  incredibly difficult and tragic day in  nova scotia and in the maritimes  on the night of april 18th frank and don  galentin  were murdered their future and their  dream home  torched a nightmare you know  it's uh again  i i haven't come to terms with it i can  honestly say  today's probably been the first day that  it's actually hit me  the loss of his mother hit hard on this  particular day which  would have been her 63rd birthday  jonathan  his fiancee abigail thompson and his  adopted sister tristina arnold spent it  sharing memories and regrets you just  wish you talked more and texts more and  visited more a few mementos  notes of condolence from canadians and  the couple's ashes are all the family  has left  everything else was incinerated in the  house fire in puerto pic  i'm a firm believer that she's with us  now and  getting more positive energy to do it  and you know  to tell the story of her life and  my dad's life  so many questions remain unanswered  about how a single gunman  could roam freely to burn to kill so  many  for so long 22 people  were massacred in 13 hours  they got the killer right here they got  him on lockdown they're going for him  right now  they got the killer right here the  rampage finally ended when mounties ran  into the murderer at this gas station by  chance and killed him  criticism of the rcmp response came  almost immediately  one of the most persistent questions is  why they didn't do more to warn the  public  it's very vague what's going on down  there but there is  for sure multiple patients down there  [Applause]  rcmp arrived in puerto pick at 10 26  on the night of april 18th but the only  alert that night  was a vague tweet about a firearms  complaint  what's more the rcmp didn't tell nova  scotians who their suspect was  until the next morning but radio  communications show they had that  information  in the early hours of their  investigation permission here is the  container  locating the rest are suspected  our suspect is clear warming in fact  a wounded witness had told mounties  almost immediately  when they first arrived on scene he had  been shot by a man named  gabe and that the suspect was driving  what looked like  a police car  [Music]  saying they don't know they didn't know  till 6am  who the shooter was meanwhile you know  there's a victim that told them  you know at 10 30 in the evening who the  who the  suspect was in the days that followed  the shootings rcmp  offered contradictory information or  none at all  on april 20th this was the response to  questions about  why the province's emergency alert  system wasn't used  it's a good question and i don't have an  answer for you at this moment  then they claimed twitter was a more  effective way of alerting people that  there was a mass  murderer on the loose we had relied on  on twitter as my colleague said because  of its  the instantaneous manner that we can  communicate say their  their tweet alone you know where's their  accountability for that  who's got what 62 year olds are on  twitter  on april 22nd here's how police  addressed questions about  why the public wasn't notified earlier  that the killer was  impersonating a police officer the  information about the vehicle  the clothing took some time to  learn from the one witness  then a couple days later a completely  different answer  fairly early into our involvement we  learned of a possible suspect  and learned the individual lived in a  home within that same community in porto  peak  there is perhaps no better example of  the rcmp's fractured response and lack  of accountability than what happened at  this fire hall on the morning of april  19th  i heard shots and then the wife started  screaming  pat mccabe and his wife lived next door  to the fire hall which was designated as  a refuge for people fleeing portapik  they saw an unmarked rcmp vehicle arrive  and then chaos two people got out one on  each side of the car left the doors open  and they started shooting from the road  the gunfire sent an emergency management  office employee  scrambling for cover by sheer luck  bullets missed him  and missed an rcmp car stationed in the  parking lot  with a mountie sitting inside either of  them  or others in the area could have easily  been killed  what i can do is i can provide you with  some general comments  for months police declined to answer  questions about what happened at the  fire hall  pending an investigation when the report  was finally released in february it made  clear that police were  totally unprepared to deal with a  suspect who was dressed  like them their radios weren't working  and rather than wait for more  information  officers started yelling at the man they  saw in the parking lot  and then opened fire the report found  the communication issues that morning  were due to the heavy volume of radio  traffic  it also found the officers had  reasonable grounds to think they saw the  gunman in the parking lot  and fired to stop him no charges were  laid  and the rcmp will only say they're  looking into the radio problem  [Music]  it's been more than 10 months since the  rcmp has answered questions about the  shootings  in december they did charge gabriel  wartman's common-law partner lisa  banfield  her brother james banfield and  brother-in-law brian brewster  with illegally transferring ammunition  to the killer  but rcmp claimed they didn't know what  wartman was going to do  on the weekend of the shootings jonathan  farrington and his fiancee say police  continue to keep them in the dark just  literally almost everything regarding  this case i've  i've got from social media from the  get-go i don't think they've actually  ever told us information that we haven't  already heard  it took 11 months for the nova scotia  government to finally introduce  legislation to make it illegal for  people to own police equipment like  cars and uniforms so far that  is the only thing the province has done  but there was a response  from the federal government last week 22  canadians were killed in the deadliest  rampage  in our country's history only days after  the nova scotia massacre prime minister  justin trudeau used nova scotia's  tragedy  to announce a long-promised piece of gun  control legislation  today we are closing the market for  military-grade  assault weapons in canada  it seemed like a pledge finally  fulfilled to victims families and  survivors of  another horrific mass shooting this  is i'm told the worst shooting disaster  in canadian history  those who escaped the infamous  polytechnic massacre have been pushing  for stronger gun laws  ever since a shooter murdered 14 women  at the montreal school in 1989. and  there is a chilling connection  to nova scotia one of the things that we  learned after the nova scotia shooting  was that  the gunman in that case had a ruger mini  14 that he used  the same weapon that was used in  polytechnic 30 years ago 31 years ago  now  how do you feel knowing that  you cannot imagine how mad i am  natalie provo was shot four times at  polytechnic  but survived she understands better than  most the h